Title: The Fireside
Order: 2
Production Code: 1x02

Radio play, Part 2 of 6. Adapts the first half of Part 2 of the novella. Running time about 16 minutes.

INT. THE LISTWELL FARM - SITTING ROOM - NIGHT, WINTER 1840

AMB: a warm interior, a clock ticking, the fire settling

MUSIC: BED IN - low cello, a hearth-warm theme, the string note from the last episode transformed

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Five years after the foregoing singular occurrence, in the winter of 1840, Mr. and Mrs. Listwell sat together by the fireside of their own happy home in the State of Ohio.

SFX: a lamp wick turned down a little

NARRATOR (V.O.) (CONT'D)
The children were all gone to bed. All was still and comfortable within; but the night was cold and dark; a heavy wind sighed and moaned sorrowfully around the house and barn.

AMB: wind outside, loud against the glass, a loose shutter clattering

SFX: dry oak leaves flung against a window

MUSIC: BED OUT

SFX: from outside, a dog's low growl

[[Mrs. Listwell]] and [[Mr. Listwell]] stop talking at once.

MRS. LISTWELL
(whisper)
What can it mean? Certainly no one can be out on such a night as this.

LISTWELL
(reassuring, a little too brightly)
The wind has deceived the dog, my dear. He has mistaken the noise of falling branches, brought down by the wind, for that of the footsteps of persons coming to the house.

SFX: the dog barks furiously, then once more, and a scuffle in frozen grass

AMB: wind, the dog's barking carrying across the yard

[[Monte]] is a brave old dog and sounds it.

LISTWELL (CONT'D)
(listening)
Our brave old Monte is on the lookout. He will not be slow in sounding the alarm.

SFX: the barking breaks off, and in its place the dog whimpering at a door, nails scrabbling on boards

LISTWELL (CONT'D)
(relieved, understanding)
Monte has made a friend.

SFX: a chair pushed back, Listwell's boots crossing the floor, the bolt drawn

SFX: the door opened, wind rushing in, lamplight throwing a shaft out into the dark

SFX: a stick tapping on the frozen doorstep, once, twice, careful

LISTWELL (CONT'D)
(calling out)
Come in, sir, don't be alarmed. You have probably lost your way.

SFX: slow, heavy footsteps, a man's, hesitating on the threshold

[[Madison Washington]] steps into the lamplight. His eyes go to the windows, the door behind him, the corners of the room, in that order.

MADISON
(a pause, then deliberate)
No, sir. I have come to ask you a greater favor.

LISTWELL
(it hits him all at once)
Oh, sir. I know not your name, but I have seen your face, and heard your voice before. I am glad to see you. I know all. You are flying for your liberty. Be seated. Be seated. Banish all fear. You are safe under my roof.

SFX: Madison takes a step back, the stick tightening in his grip, the floorboard answering

MUSIC: STING - a single plucked string, uneasy

MRS. LISTWELL
(softly, behind her husband)
Gently, now. He is afraid of us.

NARRATOR (V.O.)
The timidity and suspicion of persons escaping from slavery are easily awakened, and often what is intended to dispel the one, and to allay the other, has precisely the opposite effect. It was so in this case.

LISTWELL
(slower, quieter, gentle)
Forgive me. I should have said it more gently. Please. Sit by the fire.

AMB: the fire popping, wind outside lowering

MADISON
(measured)
Sir, you have rightly guessed. I am, indeed, a fugitive from slavery. My name is Madison. Madison Washington, my mother used to call me. I am on my way to Canada, where I learn that persons of my color are protected in all the rights of men.

SFX: Madison sits, slowly, the chair taking his weight

MADISON (CONT'D)
My object in calling upon you was to beg the privilege of resting my weary limbs for the night in your barn. The piercing cold and the frowning darkness compelled me to seek shelter; and, seeing a light through the lattice of your window, I was encouraged to come here.

LISTWELL
A resting-place, indeed, sir, you shall have; not, however, in my barn, but in the best room of my house. Consider yourself, if you please, under the roof of a friend; for such I am to you, and to all your deeply injured race.

SFX: Mrs. Listwell lifting a pot, a kettle set on iron, a cupboard opened

MUSIC: BED IN - the cello, warmer

SFX: plates set down, a knife and fork, a cup of tea poured

MADISON
(a quiet wonder, to himself)
It was the first time in my life that I had met so humane and friendly a greeting at the hands of persons whose color was unlike my own.

SFX: a fork picked up, laid down, picked up again

MRS. LISTWELL
(gently)
Eat, Mr. Washington. It is the best the house can afford.

MADISON
(quiet)
Thank you, ma'am. Thank you.

MUSIC: BED OUT

INT. THE LISTWELL FARM - SITTING ROOM - LATER

AMB: the fire lowered, the clock, the wind quieter

SFX: a chair pulled closer to the hearth

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Supper over, all doubts and apprehensions banished, the three drew around the blazing fire, and a conversation commenced which lasted till long after midnight.

MADISON
(carefully)
I was a little surprised and alarmed when I came in by what you said. Do tell me, sir, why you thought you had seen my face before. I am sure that I never was before in this neighborhood.

SFX: Listwell setting down his cup

LISTWELL
(plain, moved)
Five years ago, on a Sabbath morning, in a pine forest in Virginia, I was resting my horse at a brook. I heard a voice. I hid, and I heard a man say, "I shall be free."

AMB: wind outside, low

MADISON
(slowly)
That was you.

LISTWELL
Ever since that morning you have seldom been absent from my mind. Though I did not dare hope that I should ever see you again, from that hour your face seemed to be daguerreotyped on my memory.

SFX: Madison's breath, a long exhale

MADISON
(quiet)
I well remember that morning. And the bitter anguish that wrung my heart. I will state the occasion of it.

MUSIC: BED IN - low strings, the cello dropping to a single repeated note

INT. A PLANTATION - A TREE LIMB - MADISON'S MEMORY - DAY

AMB: the plantation, far away, crows, a mill-race running

SFX: a rope thrown over a limb, drawn tight, a man's grunt of effort

SFX: chain dragged on the ground, an iron bar clanking between ankles

MADISON (V.O.)
(telling it flatly, almost without feeling, which is how we know)
On the previous Saturday I had been tied up to the limb of a tree, with my feet chained together, and a heavy iron bar placed between my ankles.

SFX: a long cow-skin whip, drawn back, cutting the air

SFX: the whip landing, once. Then again. The count in a flat overseer's voice.

THE OVERSEER
(off, harsh, unhurried)
Hold your tongue, you impudent rascal.

MADISON (V.O.) (CONT'D)
Thus suspended, I received on my naked back forty stripes, and was kept in this distressing position three or four hours. And then I was let down only to have my torture increased; for my bleeding back, gashed by the cow-skin, was washed by the overseer with old brine, partly to augment my suffering, and partly, as he said, to prevent inflammation.

SFX: a ladle of brine dashed against skin, a man's hiss of breath through teeth

MADISON (V.O.) (CONT'D)
My crime was that I had stayed longer at the mill, the day previous, than it was thought I ought to have done. I assured my master and the overseer it was no fault of mine. No excuses were allowed.

SFX: the rope lowered, a body falling to the earth

MUSIC: BED OUT

INT. THE LISTWELL FARM - SITTING ROOM - NIGHT

AMB: the fire, the wind, the clock

SFX: Mrs. Listwell's hand over her mouth, a small sound

MADISON (CONT'D)
(his voice steady again)
Smarting still from the wounds, as well as from the consciousness of being whipped for no cause, I took advantage of the absence of my master, who had gone to church, to spend the time in the woods and brood over my wretched lot.

SFX: church bells, very distant, remembered

LISTWELL
(very quietly)
But this was five years ago. Where have you been since?

MADISON
I will try to tell you.

MUSIC: BED IN - a low pulse, slow, like walking in the dark

EXT. THE PLANTATION - NIGHT - MADISON'S MEMORY

AMB: night, clouds hiding the sky, wind in the corn, a dog's bark very far off

SFX: bare feet on a dirt road, running, then walking, then stumbling

MADISON (V.O.)
Four weeks after that Sabbath morning I gathered up the few rags of clothing I had, and started, as I supposed, for the North and for freedom.

SFX: rain beginning, thin, then steady

MADISON (V.O.) (CONT'D)
The third night after being out, a season of clouds and rain set in, wholly preventing me from seeing the North Star, which I had trusted as my guide, not dreaming that clouds might intervene between us.

SFX: a foot striking a stump, a groan, a man dropping to one knee

MADISON (V.O.) (CONT'D)
In losing my star, I lost my way. When I supposed I was far towards the North, and had almost gained my freedom, I discovered myself at the very point from which I had started.

SFX: a long exhale that is almost a laugh and almost a sob

AMB: the plantation, close now, distant voices singing in the quarters

SFX: the quarters' fire, laughter, someone playing a bone

MADISON (V.O.) (CONT'D)
Peeping through the rents of the quarters, I saw my fellow-slaves seated by a warm fire, merrily passing away the time, as though their hearts knew no sorrow. Although I envied their seeming contentment, I despised the cowardly acquiescence in their own degradation which it implied. I dared not enter the quarters, for where there is seeming contentment with slavery, there is certain treachery to freedom.

SFX: a garden fence, a hand gripping it, wet wood

MADISON (V.O.) (CONT'D)
I proceeded towards the great house, in the hope of catching a glimpse of my poor wife, whom I knew might be trusted with my secrets even on the scaffold.

SFX: a woman's quick steps in the yard, stopping

SFX: Madison stops, breath held, then lets the breath go

MADISON (V.O.) (CONT'D)
Happily for me, before the fatal moment of utter despair, my good wife made her appearance in the yard. It was she. I knew her step.

SFX: two sets of footsteps meeting, a gasp, a low cry smothered against a shoulder

[[Susan]] says nothing in this scene except his name, three times, softly.

SUSAN
(barely audible)
Madison. Madison. Madison.

MUSIC: BED OUT

EXT. THE PINE FOREST - MIDNIGHT - MADISON'S MEMORY

AMB: pines, black night, wind, the brook far down the slope

MADISON (V.O.)
She left me with the purpose of meeting me at midnight in the very forest where you last saw me. I lay among the leaves. I was strongly tempted to return again to the house of my master and give myself up; but remembering my solemn pledge on that memorable Sunday morning, I was able to linger out the two long hours between ten and midnight.

SFX: a faint rustle, then footsteps, quick, light, coming nearer

SFX: a basket set down on a log, cloth unwrapped

MADISON (V.O.) (CONT'D)
True to her word, my wife came laden with provisions, and we sat down on the side of a log, at that dark and lonesome hour of the night. I cannot say we talked; our feelings were too great for that.

AMB: wind through pine, long, with no other sound

MADISON (V.O.) (CONT'D)
We mutually determined that I should remain in the vicinity. In the dismal swamps I lived, sir, five long years, a cave for my home during the day.

MUSIC: BED IN - hollow, a wooden flute, a sound like wind in a bottle

EXT. THE DISMAL SWAMP - NIGHT - MADISON'S MEMORY

AMB: frogs, standing water, the creak of cypress, the far-off howl of wolves

SFX: a man's careful footsteps in shallow water

SFX: a wolf's howl, near, then answered by another further off

MADISON (V.O.)
I wandered about at night with the wolf and the bear, sustained by the promise that my good Susan would meet me in the pine woods at least once a week. This promise was redeemed, I assure you, to the letter.

SFX: a flint struck on a file, a bright scrape, a spark, a small flame catching in tinder

[[The Tinder-Box]] is struck once here, quietly, so that we will know its sound.

MADISON (V.O.) (CONT'D)
I had partly become contented with my mode of life, and had made up my mind to spend my days there; but the wilderness that sheltered me thus long took fire, and refused longer to be my hiding-place.

MUSIC: BED OUT

EXT. THE DISMAL SWAMP - NIGHT - THE FIRE

AMB: a low crackle, a distant roar, rising

SFX: fire catching in dry timber, a crack like a gunshot, then another

MUSIC: BED IN - low brass, a drone, the first loud sound in the drama

MADISON (V.O.)
I will not harrow up your feelings by portraying the terrific scene of this awful conflagration. There is nothing to which I can liken it. It was horribly and indescribably grand.

SFX: a tree falling, a long tearing sound, and the roar swallowing it

SFX: animals fleeing, a wolf howling, a bear bellowing, hooves, wings, small things screaming in the leaves

MADISON (V.O.) (CONT'D)
The whole world seemed on fire, and it appeared to me that the day of judgment had come. Bears and wolves, scorched from their mysterious hiding-places in the earth, and all the wild inhabitants of the untrodden forest, filled with a common dismay, ran forth, yelling, howling, bewildered amidst the smoke and flame.

SFX: running footsteps, heavy breathing, a man's coughing

SFX: the fire, closer, sparkling, creaking, cracking, curling, roaring

MADISON (V.O.) (CONT'D)
Running before it, and stopping occasionally to take breath, I looked back to behold its frightful ravages, and to drink in its savage magnificence. It was awful, thrilling, solemn, beyond compare.

SFX: buzzards and crows over the smoke, a thousand small birds rising in a cloud

MADISON (V.O.) (CONT'D)
Many a poor wandering fugitive, who, like myself, had sought among wild beasts the mercy denied by our fellow men, saw, in helpless consternation, his dwelling-place and city of refuge reduced to ashes forever.

MUSIC: BED OUT - brass cut, the fire dying under a long slow decay

MADISON (V.O.) (CONT'D)
(very quietly)
It was this grand conflagration that drove me hither. I ran alike from fire and from slavery.

AMB: the fire dwindling to a crackle, then the wind, then the fireside

INT. THE LISTWELL FARM - SITTING ROOM - NIGHT

AMB: the fire, the clock, a long silence in a quiet room

SFX: Mrs. Listwell sets down her cup, her hand trembling against the saucer

NARRATOR (V.O.)
After a slight pause, for both speaker and hearers were deeply moved by the above recital, Mr. Listwell, addressing Madison, said:

LISTWELL
(quiet)
If it does not weary you too much, do tell us something of your journeyings since this disastrous burning. We are deeply interested in everything which can throw light on the hardships of persons escaping from slavery. We could hear you talk all night.

MADISON
(slowly)
For the most part, sir, my course has been uninterrupted; and, considering the circumstances, at times even pleasant. I have suffered little for want of food; but I need not tell you how I got it.

SFX: Listwell shifts in his chair, a decision

MADISON (CONT'D)
(direct, no apology)
Your moral code may differ from mine, as your customs and usages are different. The fact is, sir, during my flight, I felt myself robbed by society of all my just rights; that I was in an enemy's land, who sought both my life and my liberty. They had transformed me into a brute; made merchandise of my body. Guided by my own necessities, and in contempt of their conventionalities, I did not scruple to take bread where I could get it.

LISTWELL
(quietly)
And just there you were right. I once had doubts on this point myself, but a conversation with Gerrit Smith put an end to all my doubts.

MUSIC: BED IN - the cello, low and warm

MADISON
I had but one narrow escape during my whole journey.

MRS. LISTWELL
(leaning forward)
Do let us hear of it.

SFX: wind at the window, a branch scratching the glass

MUSIC: STING - one low note, held

MUSIC: BED OUT

NARRATOR (V.O.)
Part Three. The Tree.

AMB: wind in a bare oak, fading to nothing
